# November 19, 2026: the District of Columbia, region dc, drawn at random by tools/choose.py. Nothing was on the map in
# the District. A first pass over its oldest standing buildings and what is left of its canals and Civil War forts:
# Rock Creek Park and Georgetown, the Fort Circle earthworks, the National Mall's lockkeeper's house, and three burial grounds.
# Facts come from sources opened in this session: Wikipedia read as raw text (wiki_raw.py), National Park Service pages
# (nps.gov/places/... and the Rock Creek Park visitor-center pages, read with page.py), HMdb marker pages (National Park
# Service signs at the kilns, the tide lock and the forts), the Trust for the National Mall, Tudor Place, Congressional
# Cemetery, the Mount Zion - Female Union Band Historic Memorial Park and the National Zoo, all read with tools/page.py.
# The NPS visitor-center pages draw their opening hours by script, so the hours inside Peirce Mill and the Old Stone House
# were not read. Brick values come from what a source says a place is built of, or from a picture opened with
# tools/brick.py look; '?' where nothing opened settled it.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
BATCH={'key':'dc_uzsg','label':'Washington, D.C.: Rock Creek, Georgetown canal and the Fort Circle','added':'2026-11-19 13:31'}
HM='https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m='
NPSP='https://www.nps.gov/places/'
ROCR=' Rock Creek Park (National Park Service) says the park is open year-round during daylight hours and closed after dark.'
CWDW=['NPS: Civil War Defenses of Washington','https://www.nps.gov/cwdw/index.htm']
PLACES=[
# ---- Rock Creek valley ----
N('peirce-mill-dc','Peirce Mill','Washington','dc','industry','standing',38.94026,-77.05190,'exact','open','walk','','1820s–1897',
  'A water-powered grist mill on Rock Creek built for the millwright Isaac Peirce. The National Park Service gives 1829; Wikipedia says either 1820 or 1829. It ground corn, wheat and rye, and its machinery followed Oliver Evans\'s automated system. It stopped in 1897 when the main shaft broke. Congress took the mill and 350 acres into Rock Creek Park in 1890; it was a tea room in the 1920s, was restored and set grinding again by 1936, ground flour for government cafeterias until 1958, was shut down in 1993, and was restored again with the Friends of Peirce Mill, reopening in October 2011.',
  'The stone mill (Wikipedia\'s 2008 photograph shows rubble-stone walls and a small red brick chimney) on its mill race at Tilden Street and Beach Drive, with the park-built dam on the creek beside it. Inside, the machinery has been restored and the Park Service runs milling demonstrations; Wikipedia says on the 2nd and 4th Saturdays, the NPS says only that the mill is open seasonally and lists events on the park calendar. Interior hours not checked.',
  'A working 1820s mill inside the city limits of the capital.',
  accn='Rock Creek Park (National Park Service), 2401 Tilden Street NW. The NPS says the grounds are open for regular park hours daily and the mill opens seasonally; its visitor-center page draws the hours by script and they were not read (hours not checked).'+ROCR+' The NPS lists bus and RV parking at the mill; a mapped lot is on the Western Ridge Trail side about 400 m north (not checked on the ground).',
  links=[W('Peirce Mill'),['NPS: Peirce Mill',NPSP+'peirce-mill.htm'],['NPS: Peirce Mill visitor center','https://www.nps.gov/rocr/planyourvisit/peirce-mill-visitor-center.htm'],['Friends of Peirce Mill','http://friendsofpeircemill.org/']],conf='mixed',
  th=[38.94364,-77.05053],thn='Rock Creek Park lot north of the mill',brick='some'),
N('joaquin-miller-cabin-dc','Joaquin Miller Cabin','Washington','dc','landmark','standing',38.96402,-77.04633,'site','view','walk','','1883',
  'A log cabin the poet Joaquin Miller built in 1883 near what is now 16th and Belmont Streets NW, and lived in until late 1885. When the land was built over in 1911 it was nearly demolished; California\'s senators had it taken apart and rebuilt in Rock Creek Park, where it was dedicated in 1912. It served as a shelter, then a concession stand, and from the 1970s a poetry series met at it until 2011.',
  'An L-shaped, one-and-a-half-storey cabin of split, saddle-notched logs with chinking, a steep cross-gabled shingle roof and a fieldstone fireplace in the middle. Wikipedia calls it the only known late 19th-century Rustic log cabin in Washington. The NPS says it is not open to the public: look from outside.',
  'A western poet\'s log cabin, moved stick by stick into the capital\'s woods.',
  accn='Rock Creek Park (National Park Service). The NPS gives its location as Picnic Grove 6, and Wikipedia as near the east bank of Rock Creek about half a mile north of Military Road. Not open inside.'+ROCR+' Nearest mapped lot not checked; the pin is the Wikipedia coordinate.',
  links=[W('Joaquin Miller Cabin'),['NPS: Joaquin Miller Cabin',NPSP+'joaquin-miller-cabin.htm']],brick='no'),
N('fort-derussy-dc','Fort DeRussy','Washington','dc','fort','traces',38.96351,-77.05094,'site','public','walk','forts','1861–1865',
  'An earthwork fort built in 1861 on a hill on the west bank of Rock Creek, to cover the approaches to Fort Stevens; the NPS says by the 4th New York Heavy Artillery, named for its colonel, Gustavus A. DeRussy (Wikipedia allows that it may be named for his father). It mounted 11 guns and mortars, among them a 100-pounder Parrott rifle, and during the Battle of Fort Stevens on July 11-12, 1864 its guns fired 109 rounds; Wikipedia says the Parrott alone got off 32.',
  'The NPS calls it well preserved: the parapet of high earth mounds with gaps where the guns stood, the deep dry ditch round it, traces of the powder magazines inside, and rifle trenches running off in both directions outside.',
  'One of the best-kept Civil War forts in the capital, in the woods a few minutes off Military Road.',
  accn='Rock Creek Park (National Park Service).'+ROCR+' An NPS sign on the paved path along Oregon Avenue NW about 250 feet north of Military Road says the earthworks are about 200 yards along the path to the right; a second NPS marker stands at the fort, reached by a trail through the woods about 200 yards north-east of Oregon Avenue and Military Road (HMdb). Parking near the sign not checked.',
  links=[W('Fort DeRussy (Washington, D.C.)'),['NPS: Fort DeRussy',NPSP+'fort-derussy.htm'],['HMdb: Fort De Russy (trail sign)',HM+'235916'],['HMdb: Fort DeRussy',HM+'20823'],CWDW],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('fort-stevens-dc','Fort Stevens','Washington','dc','fort','traces',38.96443,-77.02893,'exact','public','walk','forts','1861–1865',
  'Built in 1861 as Fort Massachusetts to guard the Seventh Street Turnpike (now Georgia Avenue), and enlarged and renamed for Brig. Gen. Isaac Stevens. Soldiers took the ground from Elizabeth Proctor Thomas, a free Black woman, and destroyed her house. Jubal Early\'s Confederates probed it on July 11-12, 1864; on July 12 Abraham Lincoln stood on the parapet and came under fire, the only time a sitting president has. The Civilian Conservation Corps restored part of the parapet and one magazine in the late 1930s.',
  'The partly restored earthwork, with a stone on the parapet marking where Lincoln stood, NPS signs and (HMdb) a scale model of the fort. The Battleground National Cemetery is half a mile north.',
  'Where a sitting president came under enemy fire.',
  accn='Civil War Defenses of Washington, Rock Creek Park (National Park Service), at 13th and Quackenbos Streets NW near Georgia Avenue.'+ROCR+' Street parking only seen on the map; a mapped lot on Quackenbos Street about 160 m east is not on park land and was not used.',
  links=[W('Fort Stevens (Washington, D.C.)'),['NPS: Fort Stevens',NPSP+'fort-stevens.htm'],['HMdb: Fort Stevens',HM+'188836'],CWDW],brick='no'),
N('battleground-national-cemetery','Battleground National Cemetery','Washington','dc','burial','standing',38.97071,-77.02669,'exact','public','drive','cemeteries,forts','1864–1936',
  'A one-acre national cemetery set up on farmland after the Battle of Fort Stevens in July 1864, for the Union dead; the NPS says forty soldiers who fell there, Wikipedia that forty were buried on the evening of July 12. Four civilians related to its first caretaker were buried later, and one veteran of the battle in 1936, which closed it. It is one of the smallest national cemeteries.',
  'Two Civil War 6-pounder smoothbore guns at the gate, regimental monuments to the 25th New York Cavalry, 98th Pennsylvania, 122nd New York and 150th Ohio, 41 marble headstones round a central flagpole, a marble rostrum, the caretaker\'s lodge with the Gettysburg Address in bronze on its side, and cast-iron tablets of "The Bivouac of the Dead".',
  '',
  accn='Rock Creek Park (National Park Service), 6625 Georgia Avenue NW, about half a mile north of Fort Stevens.'+ROCR+' Opening hours of the gate not checked. Street parking only seen on the map.',
  links=[W('Battleground National Cemetery'),['NPS: Battleground National Cemetery',NPSP+'battleground-national-cemetery.htm']],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
# ---- Georgetown and the canal ----
N('old-stone-house-georgetown','Old Stone House','Washington','dc','landmark','standing',38.90542,-77.06038,'exact','open','walk','','1765',
  'Built in 1765 for Christopher and Rachel Layman on M Street (then Bridge Street) in Georgetown, in three stages through the 18th century. The NPS calls it the oldest structure on its original foundation in Washington. It was a shop or business through the 19th and 20th centuries, a used-car dealership by 1953, when the government bought it; the Park Service stripped later additions and opened it in 1960. Local lore that Washington and L\'Enfant had met there helped save it.',
  'Walls of blue granite and fieldstone (Wikipedia), a brick third floor, oak front door, and an English garden behind on the old car lot. Wikipedia says the house is 85% original. The NPS says that now only the front room (a shop) and the kitchen exhibits are open; the upper floors are closed.',
  'The last pre-Revolution building in the capital, between the shops of M Street.',
  accn='Rock Creek Park (National Park Service), 3051 M Street NW. The NPS says the grounds are open daily sunrise to sunset; the house\'s hours are drawn by script on its page and were not read (Wikipedia gives 11 a.m. to 6 or 7 p.m. daily, not checked against the NPS). No parking of its own seen; Georgetown street parking.',
  links=[W('Old Stone House (Washington, D.C.)'),['NPS: Old Stone House',NPSP+'old-stone-house.htm'],['NPS: Old Stone House visitor center','https://www.nps.gov/rocr/planyourvisit/old-stone-house-visitor-center.htm']],conf='mixed',brick='some',hours="Grounds daily, sunrise to sunset; the house's own hours were not read"),
N('georgetown-canal-locks-1-4','C&O Canal Locks 1 to 4, Georgetown','Washington','dc','canal','standing',38.90400,-77.05830,'site','public','walk','canal','1831',
  'The first four lift locks of the Chesapeake and Ohio Canal, a staircase that lifts the canal out of Rock Creek into Georgetown. The canal opened from Little Falls to Seneca in 1830 and through Georgetown the next year; it runs 184.5 miles to Cumberland, Maryland, with 74 lift locks. The NPS signs say it was busiest from 1865 to 1880, when more than nine boats in ten reached Georgetown loaded with coal.',
  'The watered canal between brick warehouses, with the four locks stepping up from Rock Creek. The NPS says the best view of the staircase is from the bank of Rock Creek looking up through Lock 1. A replica canal boat run by Georgetown Heritage takes passengers through a lock (tickets on its site; season not checked).',
  'Mile 0 of a canal that runs 184 miles to the Alleghenies.',
  accn='Chesapeake & Ohio Canal National Historical Park (National Park Service). The towpath is a public trail; hours not checked. The NPS lists building projects in the Georgetown section, so parts of the towpath may be closed. Street parking in Georgetown only.',
  links=[['NPS: C&O Canal Lift Lock 1','https://www.nps.gov/places/000/c-o-canal-lock-1.htm'],['NPS: Georgetown (C&O Canal)','https://www.nps.gov/choh/planyourvisit/georgetownvisitorcenter.htm'],['HMdb: Georgetown and the C&O Canal',HM+'97762'],W('Chesapeake and Ohio Canal')],brick='?'),
N('aqueduct-bridge-abutment','Aqueduct Bridge abutment','Washington','dc','canal','ruins',38.90425,-77.07041,'exact','public','walk','canal','1843–1923',
  'The Aqueduct Bridge (the Alexandria Aqueduct) carried C&O Canal boats across the Potomac to the Alexandria Canal from 1843; the NPS says coal boats crossed it until 1886. Its eight stone piers later carried a wooden road bridge and an iron truss for road and trolley. It closed in 1923 when Key Bridge opened and was pulled down from December 1933, but the bases of the piers were kept to shield Key Bridge from ice. In 1889 the north arch of the Georgetown abutment was enlarged for the B&O\'s Georgetown Branch.',
  'The arched stone abutment at the Georgetown end, beside Key Bridge at the start of the Capital Crescent Trail, and in the river the stumps of the piers; the NPS says one pier still shows near the Virginia shore.',
  'A canal that crossed the Potomac on a bridge.',
  accn='National Park Service (Wikipedia says it oversees the abutment as a historic site; the NPS sign is a C&O Canal one). At the foot of the Capital Crescent Trail by Key Bridge, a public trail; hours not checked. Street parking in Georgetown only.',
  links=[W('Aqueduct Bridge (Potomac River)'),['NPS: C&O Canal Potomac Aqueduct','https://www.nps.gov/places/000/c-o-canal-potomac-aqueduct-aqueduct-bridge.htm']],brick='no'),
N('godey-lime-kilns','Godey Lime Kilns','Washington','dc','industry','ruins',38.90351,-77.05616,'exact','public','walk','','1864–1908',
  'Four wood-fired lime kilns built in 1864 by William H. Godey on the east bank of Rock Creek at the end of the C&O Canal, burning limestone into lime and plaster for building the city. The NPS sign says the stone came by canal from quarries beyond Seneca, Maryland, and that the kilns worked as late as 1908; Wikipedia says the business ran until 1907, when the wooden buildings were cleared. Two kilns were taken down for the ramps between Rock Creek Parkway and the Whitehurst Freeway; the other two were partly restored by 1967.',
  'Two stone kiln stacks (Wikipedia\'s photograph shows rough-coursed stone walls under ivy) against the slope beside the parkway ramps at the west end of L Street NW, with an NPS sign.',
  'Lime for the young capital, burned beside a freeway ramp.',
  accn='Rock Creek Park (National Park Service). HMdb says the marker is reached by a dirt path at the west end of L Street NW, by 2701 K Street NW; mind the parkway traffic.'+ROCR+' Street parking only.',
  links=[W('Godey Lime Kilns'),['HMdb: Godey\'s Lime Kilns (NPS sign)',HM+'136875']],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('c-o-canal-tide-lock','C&O Canal Tide Lock','Washington','dc','canal','traces',38.89970,-77.05712,'site','public','walk','canal','1830s',
  'The NPS signs call the Tide Lock the start of the 184.5-mile canal: boats left the Potomac through it into the Rock Creek basin, held back by a 200-foot dam across the creek mouth, and went up into the canal half a mile upstream. A skirting canal along the riverbank led on to the Washington City Canal on what is now Constitution Avenue.',
  'Two NPS signs on the Rock Creek Trail where the creek meets the Potomac, near Thompson\'s Boat Center. The signs say few signs of the old waterfront remain; what of the lock itself survives was not described in any source opened.',
  '',
  accn='Rock Creek Park (National Park Service), on the Rock Creek Trail by Virginia Avenue NW (the HMdb address is 2900 Virginia Avenue NW).'+ROCR+' Street parking only.',
  links=[['HMdb: Tide Lock and Rock Creek Basin (NPS sign)',HM+'144180'],['HMdb: Tide Lock (NPS sign)',HM+'159241']],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('mount-zion-female-union-band-cemetery','Mount Zion and Female Union Band Society Cemeteries','Washington','dc','burial','standing',38.91161,-77.05399,'site','public','walk','cemeteries','1808–1950',
  'Two adjoining burial grounds on the edge of Rock Creek in Georgetown. The eastern one began in 1808 as the Old Methodist Burial Ground of the Montgomery Street Church and from 1849 served almost only Black Georgetown; it passed to Mount Zion United Methodist Church. The western one was bought in 1842 by the Female Union Band Society, a benevolent society of free Black women, as a burying ground for Black people. Burials stopped in 1950 and both grounds were abandoned. They are believed to have served people escaping slavery on the Underground Railroad.',
  'About three and a half acres of graves on the slope above Rock Creek Park, beside the brick walls of Dumbarton House. Volunteers clean the grounds and the gravestones.',
  'The oldest Black burial grounds in Georgetown.',
  accn='Owned by the trustees of the two cemeteries; the Mount Zion - Female Union Band Historic Memorial Park, Inc. cares for them, and its page invites visitors to help keep the grounds tidy. Entrance on Mill Road NW off 27th Street NW (Wikipedia). Hours not checked. Street parking only.',
  links=[W('Mount Zion Cemetery (Washington, D.C.)'),['Mount Zion - Female Union Band Historic Memorial Park','https://www.mtzion-fubs.org/']],brick='?'),
N('tudor-place','Tudor Place','Washington','dc','landmark','standing',38.91147,-77.06304,'exact','open','walk','','1794–1816',
  'A Federal house on Georgetown Heights. The merchant Francis Lowndes built its two wings after buying the land in 1794; Thomas Peter and Martha Parke Custis Peter, a granddaughter of Martha Washington, bought it and had William Thornton, architect of the Capitol, design the centre block and the links to the wings (Wikipedia gives 1815 for its completion). The family lived there until it opened as a museum in October 1988.',
  'The house in buff stucco over brick, with its domed porch, in five and a half acres of garden. Guided tours only inside, with a show of George and Martha Washington\'s belongings; the garden is self-guided.',
  'Martha Washington\'s granddaughter\'s house, with the family\'s things still in it.',
  accn='Tudor Place Historic House & Garden, 1644 31st Street NW, a museum. Its visit page says in one place open Wednesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. and Sunday noon to 4, and in another Tuesday to Saturday 10 to 4; house by guided tour only, first at 10 a.m., last at 3 p.m.; $10 adult, $5 seniors, students, military and ages 6 to 18; garden visits self-guided. Street parking only.',
  links=[W('Tudor Place'),['Tudor Place: visit','https://tudorplace.org/visit/']],conf='mixed',brick='no',hours='Its page gives both Wednesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. with Sunday noon to 4, and Tuesday to Saturday 10 to 4. House by guided tour: first 10 a.m., last 3 p.m.'),
N('holt-house-dc','Holt House','Washington','dc','landmark','standing',38.92507,-77.04698,'exact','view','walk','','before 1814',
  'An early Neoclassical house thought to have been built before 1814, linked with Thomas Johnson, Benjamin Stoddert and others in the city\'s early years; Wikipedia counts it among the major houses of early Washington. The Smithsonian has owned it since 1890, when the National Zoo was laid out round it, and it is the oldest building the Smithsonian holds. It has stood empty since the 1980s.',
  'Load-bearing walls mostly of brick mixed with local stone, once covered in a white lime stucco. Wikipedia\'s photograph (undated) shows boarded windows and doors, a ladder against one wall, and the stucco fallen away in patches to show red brick in the walls and gables. The house is shut; whether it can be seen from the Zoo\'s paths or from Beach Drive was not checked.',
  'The Smithsonian\'s oldest building, empty for forty years inside the Zoo.',
  accn='Smithsonian Institution, on the grounds of the National Zoo (3001 Connecticut Avenue NW). The Zoo\'s page: grounds open 8 a.m. to 4 p.m., last entry 3 p.m.; entry is free but a pass must be reserved for every visitor; parking is charged. The house is closed.',
  links=[W('Holt House (Washington, D.C.)'),['Smithsonian National Zoo: visit','https://nationalzoo.si.edu/visit']],conf='mixed',brick='some'),
# ---- The Mall and Capitol Hill ----
N('lockkeepers-house-dc',"Lockkeeper's House",'Washington','dc','canal','standing',38.89183,-77.03988,'exact','open','walk','canal','1837',
  'Built in 1837 for the keeper of the lock where the C&O Canal\'s extension met the Washington City Canal on what is now Constitution Avenue; he collected tolls and kept the canal. The canal was abandoned by 1855 and the house became a squatters\' tenement, then a Park Police station from 1903. It was moved west in 1915, sat empty for some forty years, and in 2017-18 was moved again, about 36 feet south and 35 feet west, and restored by the Trust for the National Mall with the NPS; it opened in September 2018.',
  'The small house (about 540 square feet, the Trust says) with its chimneys rebuilt to the pre-1915 look, an exhibit and a six-minute film inside. The NPS page calls it a two-storey brick building, but Wikipedia\'s August 2018 photograph shows walls of coursed grey and buff stone under red brick chimneys and dormers in the roof. It is the oldest building on the National Mall.',
  'The last trace of the canal that once ran down Constitution Avenue.',
  accn='National Mall and Memorial Parks (National Park Service), southwest corner of 17th Street and Constitution Avenue NW. The NPS page: open Thursday to Sunday 11 a.m. to 3 p.m. No parking of its own; transit.',
  links=[W("Lockkeeper's House (Washington, D.C.)"),["NPS: Lockkeeper's House","https://www.nps.gov/places/lockkeeper.htm"],["Trust for the National Mall: Lockkeeper's House","https://www.nationalmall.org/lockkeepershouse/"]],conf='mixed',brick='some',hours='Thursday to Sunday 11 a.m. to 3 p.m.'),
N('christ-church-washington-parish','Christ Church, Washington Parish','Washington','dc','worship','standing',38.88172,-76.99751,'exact','view','walk','','1807',
  'An Episcopal congregation founded in 1795 that first met in a tobacco barn. The church at 620 G Street SE was built in 1807 on land given by William Prout, the first Episcopal church in the original city of Washington; it is often credited to Benjamin Latrobe, but the records point to his pupil Robert Alexander. It was enlarged from 1824 on, Union troops used its tower as a lookout in the Civil War, and in the 1920s the Victorian interior was redone in imitation stone. It owns the Congressional Cemetery.',
  'The Gothic Revival church with its pinnacled tower, built of brick (the 1806 call for builders said so) but rendered over: Wikipedia\'s photograph shows grey-brown stucco walls and a red door, and stained-glass windows in the pointed openings. The interior has been changed many times; what of it is original was not checked. Sunday services are at 9 and 11 a.m. (its page); other opening not checked.',
  '',
  accn='Christ Church, Washington Parish (Episcopal), an active church. Visitors are welcome at services; other times not checked. Near Eastern Market and the Navy Yard; street parking.',
  links=[W('Christ Church, Washington Parish'),['Christ Church, Washington Parish','https://www.washingtonparish.org/']],brick='no'),
N('congressional-cemetery','Congressional Cemetery','Washington','dc','burial','standing',38.88102,-76.97704,'site','public','walk','cemeteries','1807',
  'Washington Parish Burial Ground, founded in 1807 on four and a half acres by people of Christ Church and given to the church, which still owns it. From 1817 plots were set aside for members of Congress and officials, and Congress paid for a road from the Capitol, the public vault, the gatehouse and rows of cenotaphs. More than 65,000 people are buried or remembered there, among them John Philip Sousa and J. Edgar Hoover; Wikipedia notes many slave traders and slave owners among them. It is still an active cemetery.',
  'The rows of Latrobe cenotaphs (a square block on a plinth with a conical top) for members of Congress, many marking no grave; the public vault and the gatehouse.',
  'Where Congress buried its own, and built monuments for those buried elsewhere.',
  accn='Christ Church, Washington Parish, with the Association for the Preservation of Historic Congressional Cemetery; 1801 E Street SE, three blocks east of Potomac Avenue Metro. The cemetery\'s page: grounds open daily 7 a.m. to 7 p.m., office Monday to Friday 9 to 5; some days are marked closed to dogs. Self-guided tours.',
  links=[W('Congressional Cemetery'),['Congressional Cemetery','https://congressionalcemetery.org/']],brick='?'),
# ---- The ridge forts ----
N('battery-kemble','Battery Kemble','Washington','dc','fort','traces',38.93060,-77.09573,'site','public','walk','forts','1861',
  'A battery finished in the autumn of 1861 on Ridge Road (now Nebraska Avenue) to hold two 100-pounder Parrott rifles aimed to sweep Chain Bridge and the Virginia shore. It was named for Gouverneur Kemble of Cold Spring, New York, of the West Point Foundry. Its soldiers\' quarters were still unfinished in 1864.',
  'The parapet and gun positions, which the NPS calls fairly well preserved and visible, in the wooded Battery Kemble Park along Maddox Branch, with an NPS plaque.',
  '',
  accn='Battery Kemble Park, Rock Creek Park (National Park Service), bounded by Chain Bridge Road, MacArthur Boulevard, 49th Street and Nebraska Avenue NW. The NPS lists car parking and a trailhead; the lot used here is the mapped one on Chain Bridge Road about 270 m south of the pin.'+ROCR,
  links=[W('Battery Kemble Park'),['NPS: Battery Kemble',NPSP+'battery-kemble.htm'],CWDW],
  th=[38.92828,-77.09481],thn='Battery Kemble Park lot, Chain Bridge Road',brick='no'),
N('fort-totten-dc','Fort Totten','Washington','dc','fort','traces',38.94800,-77.00508,'site','public','walk','forts','1861–1863',
  'An earthwork fort of the northern defences, begun in August 1861 and finished by 1863, on high ground in front of the Soldiers\' Home where Lincoln spent his summers. It mounted 20 guns and mortars, among them eight 32-pounders, and its 100-pounder Parrott rifle supported Fort Stevens in July 1864. It was named for Brig. Gen. Joseph G. Totten, the Army\'s chief engineer.',
  'Wikipedia says the earthworks of the main fort are still clearly visible: the ditch, the ramparts and the walls of two magazines. The NPS sign says they are in the woods about 50 yards up the hill from it.',
  '',
  accn='Fort Totten Park (National Park Service, Rock Creek Park). The NPS sign stands on Fort Totten Drive NE north of Crittenden Street, at the north end of the loop road through the park, a short walk from Fort Totten Metro (HMdb).'+ROCR+' Parking not checked.',
  links=[W('Fort Totten (Washington, D.C.)'),['NPS: Fort Totten',NPSP+'fort-totten.htm'],['HMdb: Fort Totten (NPS sign)',HM+'2993'],CWDW],brick='no'),
N('fort-dupont-earthworks','Fort Dupont','Washington','dc','fort','traces',38.87279,-76.94029,'site','public','walk','forts','1861–1865',
  'A hexagonal earthwork begun in late 1861 to guard the Ridge Road (Alabama Avenue) crossing east of the Anacostia, each of its six sides about 100 feet long behind a deep ditch, built for 14 guns; the 1955 marker says eight guns and a mortar. It never saw battle, was recommended for abandonment in 1864 and abandoned in 1865. The NPS says people escaping slavery found safety here on the way to Washington.',
  'The NPS says the earthworks can still be traced near the picnic area on Alabama Avenue; Wikipedia says few remains of the fort are left. The bare-ground LiDAR sheet looked at for this entry shows a clear six-sided ring with works inside it, just south of the marker; not checked on the ground. The marker panels are in the woods by the Fort Circle garden and picnic area, at the north end of the one-way loop off Alabama Avenue.',
  '',
  accn='Fort Dupont Park (National Park Service), marker by 4150 Alabama Avenue SE (HMdb). Hours not checked; parking at the picnic loop not checked.',
  links=[W('Fort Dupont Park'),['NPS: Fort Dupont',NPSP+'fort-dupont.htm'],['HMdb: Fort DuPont',HM+'46425'],CWDW],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
]
